---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Inferred from "../../components/Inferred.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Routes from "../../components/Routes.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";
import Sequence from "../../components/Sequence.astro";

const API = "server/src/iki_server/api/entities.py";
const CSVS = "server/src/iki_server/api/csvs.py";
const TRAILS = "server/src/iki_server/api/trails.py";

const H = {
  short: "Four ways out, each audited",
  how: "A search's CSV, step by step",
  rules: "CSVs, audit trails, record files and bundles",
  ref: "Routes, code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      This page answers: how does data leave Iki, and what's recorded when it does? Four ways, each
      written to the audit log before the first byte leaves: a search's results as CSV (<Adr
        n="0175"
      />), one record's audit trail as CSV, a record written as a file such as GenBank (<Adr
        n="0171"
      />), and a signed bundle that carries records with their history to another deployment (<Adr
        n="0096"
      />). A single file is downloaded by a short-lived signed URL (<PageLink
        to="Where data is stored"
      />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <Sequence
      id="csv-dg"
      title="GET /api/v1/entities/search.csv"
      width={900}
      actors={[
        { id: "browser", label: "Browser" },
        { id: "api", label: "search_csv" },
        { id: "search", label: "search.py" },
        { id: "db", label: "Postgres" },
        { id: "audit", label: "Audit log" },
      ]}
      steps={[
        { id: "open", from: "browser", to: "api", label: "the grid's link: type, project, where, columns" },
        { id: "build", from: "api", to: "search", label: "check the JSON; the readable queryset" },
        { id: "first", from: "search", to: "db", label: "first page of 500, under the time limit" },
        { id: "record", from: "api", to: "audit", label: "search.exported, the search as given" },
        { id: "stream", from: "api", to: "browser", label: "header row and the first page", dashed: true },
        { id: "next", from: "search", to: "db", label: "each next page, its own time limit" },
        { id: "stop", from: "api", to: "browser", label: "or a row saying where it stopped", dashed: true },
      ]}
    >
      <div slot="open"><p>The file is named after the project and the type.</p><p><Path p={API} sym="def _download_name(" /></p></div>
      <div slot="build"><p>The columns are the grid's: ID, type, state fields, other fields, project when the search spans several, then when it last changed, its revision and its UUID (<Adr n="0175" />). How the search itself runs is on <PageLink to="Finding data" />.</p><p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/search_exports.py" /></p></div>
      <div slot="first"><p>The first page is read before anything is recorded, so a search that can't run records nothing. Each page is read in one database snapshot, so a row's values are those of the revision and time it ends with, whatever's edited while the file streams (<Adr n="0207" />).</p><p><Path p={API} sym="with snapshot(), search.time_limit():" /></p></div>
      <div slot="record"><p>The event is written in its own transaction, before any row leaves.</p><p><Path p={API} sym='"search.exported",' /></p></div>
      <div slot="stream"><p>Under ASGI each chunk is made in the request's thread. Responses are <code>no-store</code>.</p><p><Path p={CSVS} sym="thread_sensitive=True" /></p></div>
      <div slot="next"><p>Each page re-runs the readable queryset, with its references and people resolved as the reader can read them.</p></div>
      <div slot="stop"><p>A page that runs out of time ends the file with "The download stopped here", rather than failing a half-sent response.</p><p><Path p={API} sym="The download stopped here" /></p></div>
    </Sequence>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="One CSV writer" open>
        <p>
          Every CSV Iki writes goes through one writer: every cell quoted, and a cell that starts like
          a formula prefixed with <code>'</code> unless it's a number. Rows stream in pages of 500
          (<Adr n="0175" />, <Path p={CSVS} sym="QUOTE_ALL" />, <Path p={API} sym="EXPORT_PAGE = 500" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="A record's audit trail">
        <p>
          <code>/api/v1/entities/&lt;ref&gt;/revisions.csv</code> is one record's history as an inspector
          reads it: one row per changed field, the record's revisions then its registry entry's, with
          who, when, how, the agent if one acted, the reason, signatures and holds. A change made
          through a project the reader can't read shows what changed, not who or when. The export
          event records the record's revision and its entry's, read together, and the file holds
          those and earlier ones only, however late it's read (<Adr n={[25, 176, 207]} />, <Path
            p={TRAILS}
            sym="def trail_csv("
          />). Its columns:
        </p>
        <Excerpt file={TRAILS} start="^COLUMNS = \[" />
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Record files">
        <p>
          A type can declare formats its records export as. Iki writes the file from one revision's
          values, so the same revision is always the same file, and the <code>entity.exported</code>{" "}
          event keeps its SHA-256. The <code>Iki-Left-Out</code> header counts what the format couldn't
          hold, and the event lists it (<Adr n="0171" />).
        </p>
        <Excerpt file="server/src/iki_server/entities/exports.py" start="^FORMATS: dict" />
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Bundles">
        <p>
          A project's Admin exports a signed <code>.tar.gz</code> of NDJSON: people, agents, files,
          entries, entities, runs and signatures, all read at one moment. Exporting needs the
          project's Admin role, and a token the <code>admin</code> scope. At most 10,000 entities can
          be picked by hand; a search goes in as a dynamic collection. The bundle is signed with
          Ed25519, kept 7 days, and reading it checks the Admin role again each time. Importing it
          needs Org Admin as well as the project's Admin (<Adr n={[15, 96, 152]} />, <Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/bundles/exports.py"
            sym="MAX_PICKED = 10_000"
          />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/bundles/exports.py" sym="KEPT = timedelta(days=7)" />).
        </p>
        <p>
          A bundle carries each stored blob's bytes once, by SHA-256. A file registered in place goes
          as its location, object version and SHA-256 instead, and the importing deployment must be
          able to read it there (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/bundles/export.py" sym="def _file(" />).
          Offline verification checks the files a signature names against their hashes (<PageLink
            to="Versioning"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Lineage">
        <p>
          Lineage downloads as CSV too, through the same writer; it's described with the graph on <PageLink
            to="Finding data"
          />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Access during a download">
        <p>
          <Inferred why="Each page re-runs the lazy queryset; not tested directly.">
            Access revoked during a long download drops rows from later pages, since each page runs
            the readable filter again.
          </Inferred>
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Where the ADRs and the code differ">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <Adr n="0175" /> says the CSV's columns are the grid's, but the grid's Rules column has no
            CSV form, so a CSV of a <code>held</code> search doesn't say which rule held each row.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Planned">
        <p>
          Large search exports that finish in the background and can be downloaded again are on the
          roadmap after M1, with reports (<Path p="docs/roadmap.md" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <Routes tags={["bundles"]} />
    <div class="refs">
      <div>
        <h3>Where</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/search_exports.py" />: a search's CSV columns</li>
          <li><Path p={CSVS} />: the shared CSV writer</li>
          <li><Path p={TRAILS} />: the audit-trail CSV</li>
          <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/exports.py" />: record files</li>
          <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/bundles" />: bundle export and import</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Decisions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0096" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0171" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0175" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0207" title /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
